package com.instantaxtion.zcs2;

/** Help and patch notes (kept short). */
final class Texts {
    static final String VERSION = "1.3";

    static final String[] HELP = {
            "THE IDEA",
            "A whole country lives its day: a capital of about a million, other cities, dozens of towns and villages, farms, rivers, a coast, interstates and railways, army bases, an air base, a naval station and prisons. Around two million people. Start an outbreak and watch every part of it respond, or take a place in the response.",
            "MOVING ROUND",
            "Drag to move. Pinch (or the mouse wheel) to zoom, all the way from one street to the whole country. Tap anyone or anything to see who it is. The Map tool opens the layers of the national map; its first button jumps out to the whole country and back to where you were. Red arrows at the edge of the map point to the biggest fights out of view: tap one to go there.",
            "WHO YOU ARE",
            "Pick a role on the New Game screen. God has every tool. The President makes the nation's calls (alert level, martial law, mobilisation, curfew, quarantines, evacuations) and commands every formation (or hands the army to the AI general with Let the general command in the Order tool, or single formations from their sheets). The Military commander has the army, air strikes, artillery and bridges. The Incident commander runs one city. The Precinct commander sends out one station's squads. The Squad leader leads one squad. The Police officer and the Survivor are one person in the street: drag the stick to move. They shoot by themselves at the dead in reach; the Shoot switch says which: Firing, or Hold fire (to slip past quietly). They can't fast-forward, but while none of the dead are near, Skip ahead runs time on as fast as the phone can, and stops when they come close. The AI runs everything you don't.",
            "WHAT YOU SEE",
            "Close in: every person, car and shot. The living are warm colours, police blue, soldiers khaki, the dead grey-green with a red rim. Further out: people as dots over a glow where crowds are thick, cream for the living and red for the dead. From high up: the infection as a red bleed, hordes on the march, refugee columns on the roads, formations as unit symbols with arrows and front lines, state borders, the towns with how many live there and how many of the dead. At night only powered places have lights. The ? button explains the marks. The Map tool's layers show utilities (power, water, phones), supplies, public trust and fear, forces and fronts, where people died and where the dead were thickest.",
            "PEOPLE AND THE DEAD",
            "Most of the people are indoors and counted by the sector, not drawn; they come out onto the streets where something is happening, and go back in when it's over. The dead do the same: a town nobody is watching can still fall.",
            "THE RESPONSE",
            "People call 911. Police wait at their stations and come out as squads when they're sent. When a town has too many of the dead, its police hold a cordon and the army goes in. The army moves as formations on the national map: battalions, armour, artillery, marines, Guard brigades. As the alert level rises, help arrives by the interstates.",
            "THE TOOLS",
            "Along the bottom (down the left side when the phone is sideways). Each tool says what a tap does in a line above its options. Strikes are aimed first: a reticle shows where it lands, then Fire or Cancel. Orders: tap a squad or formation, then where it should go; a flag marks the spot. The Dead, Police and Army tools offer every kind in the game's content (a mod's own too): officers, soldiers, vehicles, and whole squads that come with their vehicles. The Dead tool paints from one to a quarter of a million; past a thousand and a half, the rest go into the streets' counts.",
            "ORDERS",
            "Pick a squad with the Order tool, then: Move, a Route of points (tap them, then Go), Fall back (they don't stop to fight on the way), a Cordon line between two taps, Clear area (they hunt the dead in it, then hold), or Escort someone or a vehicle. Its stance: Normal, Hold fire (only when the dead are on them) or Aggressive (they close in on the dead near them). Mount up keeps them in their vehicle. A formation can Attack, Defend, Screen, Withdraw or Dig in. Every order shows as a line and flags on the map. As God, tap any vehicle with the Order tool (or Orders on its sheet): go here, orbit, attack an area, return; Take the wheel drives it with the stick. Control on a person's sheet lets you move anyone with the stick; Release gives them back.",
            "BUILDING AND CLEARING",
            "The Build tool: barricades and walls the dead must smash through, sandbags (cover for the armed behind them), razor wire (slows the dead and cuts them), machine-gun nests (a gunner who stays put and fires along an arc), ammunition crates and medical kits (they restock and patch up the armed near them), command posts (steadier nerves round them). God can also lay roads, build houses, clear rubble and trees, and put people and civil vehicles on the street. The Clear tool drags the dead away, or clears wrecks, blood and bodies, barricades and defences, everyone on the streets, or a town's counted dead.",
            "THE RULES",
            "Rules on the New Game screen (and in God's pause menu during a game): how fast the dead are and how easily they infect, how strong the forces are, civilians on the streets or not, how early people flee, outside help, the weather and the time of day. Replays and saves keep them.",
            "TIME",
            "An hour passes every minute at 1x. 1h/s, 6h/s and 1d/s fast-forward through days and weeks: everyone folds into the counts and fights are settled by the numbers until you slow down again. Seasons and weather matter: the cold slows the dead, the heat rots them, rain and fog shorten sight.",
            "CAMPAIGNS AND YOUR OWN",
            "Campaigns: hold the capital for 30 days, evacuate the coast, retake a fallen state, protect the capital from the hordes. How a campaign stands, and the days left, is the line under the top bar (and in the pause menu). Make your own with the editor (on any map, a national one from a code too), in five tabs. Start: where it starts (a place you tap, or one of content.ini's origins), how many, hordes, the strain, season and role. Places: tap the map to put down more outbreak points, squads and defences (Undo takes the last back). Rules: the rules sheet. Goals: a campaign goal, or your own: survive N days, keep a town above N%, kill all the dead, lose no more than N. Triggers: when (a time, more than N dead in a town, a town falls, a facility lost), do (a horde here, an air strike, send a formation, the weather, a message). Triggers fire the same way in every replay.",
            "REPLAYS AND CHALLENGES",
            "Every game records what you did. Save a replay from the pause menu and it plays the whole outbreak back from its start, exactly. Under the map while it plays: tap anywhere on the scrubber to go there (back is quick: the replay keeps snapshots as it plays, marked under the track), -1h, play or pause, Step (a game minute), the speed. To direct it: move the camera, +Key keeps where it is at that moment (a yellow dot on the track), Path glides the camera through the keys, and Cut saves the replay with its camera path as a director's cut. The timelapse shows the country hour by hour. A challenge code (map, seed, scenario, role) lets a friend play the same game; your best for each code is kept.",
            "MODS",
            "Everything in the game is in content.ini: people, weapons, vehicles, the dead, squads, formations, scenarios, the rules, and also the name lists, the kinds of building and their roofs, the map presets, where an outbreak can start and who comes from outside. Settings, Mods: Import a content.ini from your phone (it is checked first, and a mistake is shown with its line), Share the one in use, or go back to the built-in one."
    };

    static final String[] NOTES = {
            "1.4  Your tools, and why things happened",
            "- Replays you can scrub: tap anywhere on the track to go there, back an hour, step a minute, play faster. The replay keeps snapshots as it plays, so going back is quick, and it still comes out exactly the same.",
            "- Direct a replay: keyframes for the camera (where it looks and how close, at a moment), a smooth path through them, and a director's cut that saves the path with the replay.",
            "- The editor has tabs: more outbreak points, squads and defences put down on the map, the rules sheet, strains, goals of your own (survive N days, keep a town above N%, kill all the dead, keep losses under N) and triggers (at a time, or when a town has N dead, falls, or a facility is lost: a horde, a strike, a formation sent, the weather, a message).",
            "- Modding as data: names, buildings and their roofs, map presets, outbreak origins and the army sent from outside are now in content.ini. Import and share a content.ini from Settings; mistakes are shown with their line.",
            "- Build: barricades, walls, sandbags, razor wire, machine-gun nests, ammunition crates, medical kits and command posts; for God also roads, houses, clearing the ground, and people and civil vehicles on the street.",
            "- Richer orders: stance (hold fire, aggressive), routes of waypoints, falling back, cordon lines, clearing an area, escorting, mounting up; formations attack, defend, screen, withdraw or dig in. Each order shows on the map.",
            "- Vehicles under your orders: go, orbit, attack an area, return, or take the wheel and drive with the stick. God can take over anyone and move them, then give them back.",
            "- The palettes are built from the game's content, a mod's included: every kind of the dead, every officer, soldier, vehicle and squad (squads come with their vehicles). Brushes of 10,000, 50,000 and 250,000 dead.",
            "- A rules sheet in the New Game screen and God's pause menu; the Clear tool clears wrecks, blood, barricades, everyone, or a town's dead. All of it is recorded, so replays stay exact.",
            "- The numbers panel (the chart button under the ?): kills by side, kills a minute, the most of the dead at once, open calls, help from outside, a sparkline of the dead and the top three fighters. Police and soldiers are counted two ways, out in squads and in all, and the top bar, the panel, the report and its key agree.",
            "- Callouts on the map where things happen: Officer down, Barricade broken, Bridge blown, Horde sighted, a town fallen. They fade, the important ones first, and never land on a label.",
            "- Every fighter keeps their last ten seconds: what they were doing, the dead round them, who was with them, ammunition and health. Their card shows it as a strip, and when they fall it says why (\"out of ammo, surrounded by 6, fell back too late\"), on the card and in the report's fallen.",
            "- A town that falls or is given up says why: it lost its police, the phones were down, a horde arrived, no army was sent, the evacuation failed. See it on the town's card and in the report.",
            "- The after-action report tells the story: the decisive moments, a map of how it ended, every town the dead reached (held or lost, when, what it cost, why) and every formation (held or broke, and why).",
            "- Fast-forward slows to 1x for big events (a horde near a town, a facility lost, a new strain, a blackout) and offers to take you there. Settings: Slow down for big events.",
            "- What the simulation knew, now drawn: safe points for the survivor (and on the Forces layer), drones orbiting, marksmen on their perches, the precinct's cordon and how long its calls have waited, and what each fighter is doing on their card.",
            "1.3  A country that makes sense, and a look that reads",
            "- Wars at the scale of a country: a small outbreak is put down within a day; a big one is a war of weeks that can be won or lost. Bites indoors, turning and fights by the numbers are paced over hours, and ammunition comes from depots and escorted convoys.",
            "- Things come down again: the alert is lowered a step at a time, curfews, shelter orders, evacuations and quarantines are lifted, outside units go home, people return to their towns, and a town clear of the dead is declared safe with what it cost.",
            "- Officials believe a reported picture that lags behind the truth. Trust follows how the response goes, and decides how well people keep their doors shut.",
            "- Evacuations move as fast as the roads allow, to towns with room, and refugees are counted once. Facilities fall and are retaken; forward bases only go up behind the line.",
            "- The general plans for towns, hordes and nests alike. As President or Military commander you command the army; Let the general command (the Order tool) hands it over, and any formation can be released to the general or taken back.",
            "- Campaigns are judged fairly from the first count, with their progress under the top bar and in the pause menu. The report says where people were lost. The news says things once and adds up the rest.",
            "- In the street: cars turn corners and get round jams, squads come out on open ground, stand down and go home, nobody rides off in an empty vehicle, and people run from all the dead near them.",
            "- A new look: muted ground, streets with markings, buildings with roofs and shadows, trees, real nights with lit windows where the power is on, smoke, rain, fog, snow and lightning. Everything on the map is sized for the phone's screen, with outlines; labels and the radio no longer land on each other.",
            "- A compact top bar, tools with icons, palettes that say what a tap does, an inspector with bars, a sideways layout, aimed and confirmed strikes, arrows to fights out of view; the whole country fills a tall screen and can be panned.",
            "- The President can lower the alert, lift martial law and end mobilisation as well as declare them. Formations can be tapped at any zoom. A new game starts on your own city, station, squad or person, with a button to the outbreak.",
            "- The officer and the survivor can skip ahead while none of the dead are near. Your best on a challenge is one whole game; results show minutes; the radio log shows whole messages; the editor works sideways; challenge codes say when a role or scenario can't be used.",
            "- Sturdier on phones: an error no longer closes the game (it saves and offers the menu), a damaged save is set aside, loads can be cancelled with back, the renderer setting is honoured, and the game saves every three minutes.",
            "- Cars and trucks never sit on top of each other: they wait their turn at junctions and each parks in a place of its own at the kerb. Saves from 1.2 still load, and friends' result codes from 1.2 read right. The autosave no longer stutters, and the close-up map paints faster.",
            "1.2  Together",
            "- Pass-and-play: up to four players share a game, each in a role (President, Military commander, Incident commander, Precinct commander, Squad leader). Players in the pause menu; the P button hands over.",
            "- Friends' results: share your result on a challenge as a code, add theirs, and see who did best on the report.",
            "- Ships into the port and cargo planes into the airfields on a schedule; freight trains between cities with stations for long hauls of anything.",
            "- Troop trains: formations travel by rail on long journeys, and some arrive by train over the border.",
            "- Barricades close roads to vehicles: they stop and go round.",
            "- Survivor settlements farm, trade food for ammunition by caravan, and defend themselves.",
            "1.1  Play at any level",
            "- Play as God, the President, the Military commander, an Incident commander, a Precinct commander, a Squad leader, a Police officer or a Survivor: each with its own powers.",
            "- Country-scale campaigns: hold the capital for 30 days, evacuate the coast, retake a state, protect the capital.",
            "- New starts: a ship in the port, somewhere random.",
            "- Director mode: replays of whole outbreaks, a timelapse of the country, heatmaps.",
            "- Challenge codes, and your best for each.",
            "- A scenario editor; your own content.ini in the app's files replaces the built-in one.",
            "- Map layers: infection, utilities, supplies, trust and fear, forces and fronts, deaths, peak infection. State borders, populations, unit symbols with arrows.",
            "1.0  The sequel",
            "A new game, built from the ground up: a country of about two million people, cities, towns, villages, farms, rivers, a coast, interstates, railways and bridges, simulated by the sector and in person where it matters; formations, hordes, refugees, power, fuel, the government and the news; weather and seasons; fast-forward to weeks."
    };
}
